 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 544335,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A student finds the number of edges in a complete graph by taking half the number of vertices, then subtracting one from the number of vertices, then multiplying these two values together. Will the student get the correct result?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>If <i>n</i> is the number of vertices in a complete graph, then each vertex is connected to <i>n</i> – 1 other vertices (i.e., has degree <i>n</i> – 1).</p>\n<p>The total number of degrees for the graph is thus <span data-math=\"n\\left( {n - 1} \\right)\"></span>.</p>\n<p>By the Sum of Degrees Theorem, <span data-math=\"{\\text{the number of edges  =  }}\\frac{{{\\text{sum of degrees}}}}{2} = \\frac{{n\\left( {n - 1} \\right)}}{2}\"></span>.</p>\n<p>The last expression is equivalent to what the student said. <span data-math=\"\\frac{n}{2}\\left( {n - 1} \\right)\"></span></p>\n"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "Yes. If <i>n</i> is the number of vertices in a complete graph, the number of edges is <span data-math=\"\\frac{{n(n - 1)}}{2} = \\frac{n}{2}(n - 1)\">\\frac{{n(n - 1)}}{2} = \\frac{n}{2}(n - 1)</span> which is half the number of vertices times one less than the number of vertices."
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
